ctly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186567056 "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $k=2$ then\n\n$$\ndet\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }k & 2\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2=0,\n$$\n\nbut  according to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186567074 "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186567077 "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186567100 "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore only statements I is true."] [17592186573259 "We write down $P$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $P$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[P\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}-3 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186573266 "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186573325 "We write down $H$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $H$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[H\\,|\\,I] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${H}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{H}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186621814 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."] [17592186621873 "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -4-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 7 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-(7)(1)) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -15) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
